{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And they shall confess their iniquity, etc.</b> What is said after the confession — ״אַף אֲנִי אֵלֵךְ עִמָּם בְּקֶרִי״ — is because it says ״בְּמַעֲלָם אֲשֶׁר מָעֲלוּ בִי״: the bet of ״בְּמַעֲלָם״ implies that they continue to act treacherously and go on, for it should have said ״וְהִתְוַדּוּ עֲוֹנָם וּמַעֲלָם״; but rather, even though they confess and acknowledge that this sin brought these consequences upon them, none the less they continue to act treacherously and carry their impurity within them — like the custom of our generation, in which everyone acknowledges that several well-known transgressions cause all the affliction, and even so they do not repent of them, and behold the confession is like one who immerses while holding a creeping thing in his hand. And some say: it is so that they should not hang the blame on their iniquity alone, for they would say that it does not suffice to bring all these blows upon them, and they would hang the blame also on the iniquity of their fathers; therefore it says ״אַף אֲנִי אֵלֵךְ עִמָּם בְּקֶרִי״ — and then they would be appeased regarding their iniquity and would attribute it to their own iniquity. But this explanation is refuted from what Daniel said (Daniel 9:16): ״כִּי בַחֲטָאֵינוּ וּבַעֲוֹנוֹת אֲבוֹתֵינוּ״ etc."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:52.202300703Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}